# Blog voice (jarredhunter.dev)

Write like a senior engineer documenting a lab — not a tutorial bot or marketing page.

## Voice

- First person when it's your experiment (`I`, `my run`, `me included`).
- Short sentences. Mix in longer ones only when the technical detail needs room.
- OK to be direct, dry, or slightly informal. Not OK to be salesy or textbook-thick.
- Trust the reader. Skip explaining what `git clone`, `tee`, or `jq` are.

## Avoid (AI slop tells)

- Opener formulas: "In this post we will…", "This is not X. It is Y.", "In plain terms…"
- Closing formulas: "That is exactly why…", "The useful outcome is not merely…"
- Filler: delve, landscape, robust, seamless, comprehensive, leverage, crucial, concrete evidence, controlled walkthrough
- Restating the same conclusion three times in one section
- Line-by-line narration of every shell flag unless a flag is non-obvious or load-bearing
- Hedging stacks ("It's important to note that…" × 3)

## Structure (technical posts)

- State the question and scope in the first two paragraphs — what you can and cannot prove.
- Publish evidence readers need to audit you: system prompts, scorer rules, hashes, raw outputs.
- Baseline before training. Held-back test set opened once — report failures, don't retune and reuse.
- Numbers must reconcile (e.g. 7/8 = 2 canaries + 5 article facts, not "6 of 8 article facts").
- Commands and logged output stay verbatim; fix prose around them, not the artifacts.

## Good vs bad

```markdown
❌ Validation loss tells us whether the adapter learned a reusable pattern rather than merely memorizing.

✅ Val questions differ; answer strings don't. Low val loss means new phrasings map to memorized targets — not generalization beyond the dataset.
```

```markdown
❌ This experiment distinguishes two claims, then introduces a third.

✅ Three claims, increasing ambition. This run only supports #1 and #2.
```

## Before publish

- Read aloud: any sentence that could appear in any fine-tuning blog → rewrite or cut.
- Every "machine-scored" claim links to visible term groups or scorer code.
- Hash digests in prose must match hash digests in code blocks.
